title: Ref translation during sync
tags: Sync
kind: reference
summary: A reference names a uuid of its own repository; carried to the other side it names the counterpart — the linked record first, else the record at the same path, made there when the run needs it.

A `ref`, a `tree_ref` and a `hierarchy_ref` hold uuids local to their repository. Written on the
other side, each names the referenced record's ''counterpart'':

* ''`ref`'' — the record it is linked to; with no link, the record at the referenced record's position (its first `tree_ref` or `hierarchy_ref` path) on the other side.
* ''`tree_ref`'' — the name is kept; the parent is the record it is linked to, else the record at the parent's path ''in that field's forest'' on the other side. A linked parent is followed through its renames; an unlinked one is found by its path — which is how `category = "music/jazz"` reaches the other side though `music` itself is out of scope.
* ''`hierarchy_ref`'' — the same, row by row: each parent translated ([[Hierarchy references]]). A parent made on the other side is made a `hierarchy_ref` node.

When nothing holds that path yet, the ''run'' makes it — the record and the
ancestors it lacks, top-down, as nodes carrying only their position; a directory
made that way is refreshed once the run's file operations have put it on disk. The
plan only looks. A referenced record with no link and no position has no
counterpart: the linking phase links such a target before anything is written
([[Matching records across repositories]]). A value that still has none at run time
— its target deleted meanwhile, say — leaves that field out, with a warning, and the
link uncommitted.

`externalref` and `refbase` values already say which repository they mean: they
are copied as they are.

The snapshot keeps both perspectives of each reference ([[The sync database]]):
comparing a side with its own past needs no translation, and a link deleted or made
since the last sync cannot change what that past was. Translation is needed only to
compare the two sides now, and to write.
